tcp: never acknowledge to the tap beyond what the tap has sent

cogbox runs passt under an LD_PRELOAD shim (zig/src/netfilter/main.zig,
doRemappedConnect) that, for a remapped TCP flow, drives a 13-byte SOCKS5
CONNECT handshake (zig/src/socks5/main.zig, handshake) on passt's own flow
socket inside connect(), before passt writes the first guest byte to it.
The peer acks those 13 bytes like any other, so tcpi_bytes_acked on that
socket overstates the guest's data by 13 for the life of the flow.

passt 2026_07_16's tcp_update_seqack_wnd() (the bandwidth-delay
heuristic taken when the kernel reports tcpi_bytes_acked and
tcpi_delivery_rate, the flow is not LOCAL, closing or low-RTT, and the
sndbuf is not far above the bandwidth * delay product) derives the ACK
it sends the guest as tcpi_bytes_acked + seq_init_from_tap, i.e. 13
past the guest's snd_nxt.
The guest (Linux) treats an ACK for unsent data as invalid and discards
the segment, and passt's never-backwards guard right below then pins the
bad value, so every later ACK is discarded too: the guest's TLS handshake
stalls until its retransmit timer gives up. Observed on the terminate
tier as ~25% of HTTPS connections hanging whenever the ClientHello
exceeds one MSS (a stock TLS 1.3 hello with post-quantum key shares).
2025_09_19's variant of the heuristic never took this branch with the
~87 KB default sndbuf; the 2026_07_16 one does.

Clamp the derived ACK to seq_from_tap before the never-backwards guard.
seq_ack_to_tap <= seq_from_tap already holds from connection setup
(tcp_conn_from_tap / tcp_conn_from_sock_finish set them equal), so the guard
cannot re-raise it above the clamp. For an unmodified socket the clamp
is a no-op: the kernel cannot have acked more than passt wrote.

Upstream candidate for passt-dev: the same overstatement arises for any
wrapper or proxy that speaks on the flow socket before passt does.

--- a/tcp.c
+++ b/tcp.c
@@ -1189,6 +1189,14 @@
 		                       conn->seq_init_from_tap;
 	}
 
+	/* Never acknowledge beyond what the guest has sent: bytes the peer acked
+	 * that did not come from the tap (e.g. a proxy handshake written on this
+	 * socket by a wrapper) would otherwise produce an ACK for unsent data,
+	 * which the guest discards along with every later segment.
+	 */
+	if (SEQ_GT(conn->seq_ack_to_tap, conn->seq_from_tap))
+		conn->seq_ack_to_tap = conn->seq_from_tap;
+
 	/* It's occasionally possible for us to go from using the fallback above
 	 * to the tcpi_bytes_acked method.  In that case, we must be careful not
 	 * to let our ACKed sequence go backwards.
